I've written lengthier remarks that  develop these subjects a little bit more  precisely and these are going to be  published by the Kirby sent center on  their site and a monograph produced and  in those I have a number of  recommendations concerning the  procedures of the Senate Judiciary  Committee and holding its confirmations  but most of all what I want to do by  working with Hillsdale College in the  kirby center is communicate to the  groups of people who may not be part of  the inside the the inner constitutional  sanctum what is at stake right now so  hopefully there will be more interest  communicated to their elected officials  and eventually the elected officials  particularly those in the United States  Senate and participate in this process  might take an interest in trying to  focus the confirmation hearings on these  matters I mean have been many very  disappointing confirmation hearings in  the past I thought the one with regard  to justice Sotomayor was actually a good  step in the right direction but i think  we continue to have to encourage members  of the Senate to devote even more focus  to constitutional issues I guess most of  all what I'd like in the confirmation  process would be an increasing focus not  just on judge Smith or Judge Jones who's  actually before the committee but I wish  these would be used increasingly as an  opportunity to assess the health of the  Constitution and to analyze the the  great constitutional debates of the time  to try to inquire of judicial nominees  what they think about these issues I  think there's a real misunderstanding on  the part of many senators as to what  constitutes an appropriate kind of  inquiry and many people have a lot of  confusion about that I think it's all  together appropriate to ask questions  about how judicial candidate proposes to  resolve a constitutional dispute that is  what they would look to what  the evidence that they believe would be  appropriate to consider in that process  I don't believe its proper to ask how  would you decide a particular precise  case in the future but i do believe its  proper to ask them questions about  larger contra constitutional  controversies what do you think about a  colorblind constitution this has been a  matter of debate in the United States  for the past 30 years what do you think  about a colorblind constitution what do  you think about the debate that  Professor Code defines between the  nationalist and the trans nationalist I  think these are proper entirely proper  debates now the advice and consent  process is the choke point of our  constitutional process once a judge  judicial nominee gets beyond that choke  point he's on the bench for the rest of  his life and I think it's entirely  proper for senators and I'm talking  about senators of both parties inquiring  of judicial nominees of both parties  it's entirely proper to inquire just  what kind of custodian a judicial  nominee will become if he is blessed  with this opportunity to define our  Constitution for the rest of his or her  life and I don't think senators need to  be apologetic about that I think they  need to understand this is one of the  most important responsibilities that  they carry out especially those on the  Senate Judiciary Committee
 

